<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince Gong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1833">1833</a>–<a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Yixin, Prince Kung · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aisin-gioro">House of Aisin Gioro</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yixin, Prince Gong, was a Qing imperial prince and statesman, the sixth son of the Daoguang Emperor and a leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement</a>. Left behind with full powers when the Xianfeng Emperor fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1860, he signed the Convention of Peking with the British, French and Russians. In 1861 he set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen">Zongli Yamen</a>, in effect a foreign ministry, and joined the Empresses Dowager Ci'an and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Cixi</a> in a coup against the late emperor's regents, serving as prince-regent until 1865. Cixi dismissed him in 1884 and recalled him in 1894.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-gong.jpg" alt="Prince Gong" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felice Beato · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Felice_Beato_(British,_born_Italy_-_Portrait_of_Prince_Kung,_Brother_of_the_Emperor_of_China,_Who_Signed_the_Treaty_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yixin was born on 11 January 1833 into the Aisin Gioro clan as the sixth son of the Daoguang Emperor and the third son of Imperial Noble Consort Jing, of the Khorchin Mongol Borjigit clan. He studied in the imperial library and practised martial arts with his fourth brother Yizhu, and the emperor named the spear and sword movements he created. When Daoguang fell critically ill in 1850, his secret edict named Yizhu as successor and made Yixin a first-rank prince, though a rumour held that the edict had been tampered with. Yizhu became the Xianfeng Emperor and gave him the title Prince Gong of the First Rank.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1860, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-opium-war">Second Opium War</a>, British and French forces closed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and the Xianfeng Emperor fled to Rehe, appointing Prince Gong Imperial Commissioner with full authority. On 24 October 1860 he signed the Convention of Peking with British, French and Russian officials, then asked to be punished for signing an unequal treaty; the emperor replied that there was no need. In 1861 he set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen">Zongli Yamen</a>, the de facto foreign affairs ministry, and in 1862 founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwen-guan">Tongwen Guan</a> to teach technology and foreign languages.</p><p>After Xianfeng died in August 1861, Prince Gong, at odds with the eight conservative regents named in the will, joined the Empress Dowagers Ci'an and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Cixi</a> in the Xinyou Coup and had them arrested. The Tongzhi Emperor made him Prince-Regent. In 1864 Qing forces ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a>, and the court praised his leadership. In April 1865 an official accused him of monopolising power and taking bribes, and the empress dowagers stripped him of the regency. He kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen">Zongli Yamen</a> and a seat on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a>, and supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zhidong">Zhang Zhidong</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Cixi</a> came to distrust him, and in 1884, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> could not decide whether to intervene when the French invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, she removed him from all positions. His brother Yixuan, Prince Chun, replaced him. In October 1894, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a>, she recalled him to head the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen">Zongli Yamen</a> and the Admiralty and gave him command of the capital's defences. He died in May 1898 aged 67 by East Asian reckoning. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a> attended the funeral and gave him the posthumous name Zhong. His tomb is in Changping District, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, and his former residence in Xicheng District is a museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prince Gong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His younger half-brother, <b>Prince Gong</b>, left behind to negotiate, signed the Convention of Peking, which confirmed the Treaties of Tianjin of 1858: foreign envoys would reside in Beijing, more ports were opened, and foreigners and missionaries could travel inland.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1875" aria-label="Beijing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Prince Gong"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4765 4154h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4765 4154h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1875">What was here in 1875? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
